<h1>Likutay Halachos</h1>
<p class="subtitle">Choshen Mishpat &mdash; Laws of Fraud (<em>Onaah</em>)</p>

<h2>Onaah 1</h2>

<div class="verse-block">
<em>&ldquo;The [measure of] fraud is a sixth&rdquo;</em>
</div>

<p class="no-indent">Based on what Rabbainu, may his light shine, wrote in <span class="bracket">[Likutay Moharan I]</span> Torah 280, on the verse <span class="verse">&ldquo;When you go out&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">[Deuteronomy 21:10]</span> — regarding the aspect of <span class="key-term">masa umattan be&rsquo;emunah</span> <span class="bracket">[conducting business with faithfulness]</span>. For the purpose of <span class="key-term">masa umattan</span> <span class="bracket">[commerce]</span> is to refine the <span class="key-term">nitzozos</span> <span class="bracket">[holy sparks]</span>, and this is accomplished through <span class="key-term">emunah</span> <span class="bracket">[faith]</span> — by conducting the business transaction with faithfulness; see there. And one must attach the inner dimension of one&rsquo;s thought, during the time of <span class="key-term">masa umattan</span>, to the Torah. And through this, <span class="key-term">emunah</span> receives the power to refine the <span class="key-term">nitzozos</span>; see there.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Chochmah</span>, <span class="key-term">Binah</span>, <span class="key-term">Da&rsquo;as</span> <span class="bracket">[Wisdom, Understanding, Knowledge]</span> of <span class="key-term">Asiyah</span> <span class="bracket">[the World of Action]</span> ascending to <span class="key-term">Yetzirah</span> <span class="bracket">[the World of Formation]</span>, which is <span class="hebrew-letter">ו</span> <span class="bracket">[<span class="key-term">vav</span>]</span>, which is the Torah. <span class="bracket">(See there, all of this, well.)</span></p>

<p>And behold, <span class="key-term">Yetzirah</span> is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Matatron</span>, which is <span class="hebrew-letter">ו</span> <span class="bracket">[<span class="key-term">vav</span>, numerical value = 6]</span>, which is the aspect of the <strong>six orders of the <span class="key-term">Mishnah</span></strong> that clarify the law: <em>kosher</em> and <em>invalid</em>, <em>impure</em> and <em>pure</em>, <em>forbidden</em> and <em>permitted</em>. And therefore, from there <span class="key-term">emunah</span> receives the power to refine the <span class="key-term">nitzozos</span>. For the refinement of the <span class="key-term">nitzozos</span> is the aspect of separating the good from the bad, as is known. And this is accomplished specifically through the Torah — that is, through clarifying the law of what is <em>kosher</em> and <em>invalid</em> etc. — through this one is able to separate the good from the bad, which is the refinement of the <span class="key-term">nitzozos</span>, as is brought regarding this in the words of Rabbainu, may his light shine, Torah 8 <span class="bracket">[Likutay Moharan I:8]</span>.</p>

<p>And behold, when one <strong>defrauds and deceives</strong> one&rsquo;s fellow, one blemishes <span class="key-term">emunah</span>, and the <span class="key-term">masa umattan</span> is not conducted with faithfulness, for one has cheated and deceived one&rsquo;s fellow. And therefore the measure <span class="bracket">[of fraud]</span> is <strong>a sixth</strong> — the aspect of <span class="hebrew-letter">ו</span> <span class="bracket">[<span class="key-term">vav</span> = 6]</span>, which is the aspect of the Torah from which <span class="key-term">emunah</span> receives <span class="bracket">[its power]</span>, as mentioned above.</p>

<p>And therefore: <strong>exactly a sixth — one returns the overcharge (<span class="key-term">onaah</span>); less than a sixth — it is not considered at all; more than a sixth — the transaction is void (<span class="key-term">bitul mekach</span>)</strong>. For the blemish of <span class="key-term">onaah</span> is that one <strong>mixes good with bad</strong> — that is, <span class="key-term">emunah</span> is blemished and does not refine the <span class="key-term">nitzozos</span>, as mentioned above. And this is the aspect of blemishing the <span class="hebrew-letter">ו</span>, which is the Torah, the aspect of <span class="key-term">Matatron</span> as mentioned above, which is the aspect of <em>kosher</em> and <em>invalid</em> etc., as mentioned above.</p>

<p>And therefore, as long as it is <span class="bracket">[exactly]</span> <strong>a sixth</strong> — it is returnable. For the aspect of <span class="key-term">Matatron</span>, the aspect of the Torah mentioned above, is divided into six — the aspect of <em>kosher</em> and <em>invalid</em> etc., as mentioned above. It turns out that when the blemish reaches only the aspect of a sixth portion and has not yet reached the aspect of <em>more than a sixth</em> — that is, <span class="bracket">[it has not reached the point of]</span> reversing from <em>kosher</em> to <em>invalid</em> — therefore it is returnable.</p>

<p>But when the blemish is in the aspect of <strong>more than a sixth</strong>, then it is the aspect of <strong>mixing good with bad</strong> — for it reverses from <em>kosher</em> to <em>invalid</em> etc. For in the aspect of <span class="key-term">Matatron</span>, the aspect of the Torah mentioned above, there are only six aspects — which are six portions alone: <em>kosher</em> and <em>invalid</em>, <em>impure</em> and <em>pure</em>, <em>forbidden</em> and <em>permitted</em>; and there cannot be more portions than these, for as soon as something departs from <em>kosher</em>, it is immediately <em>invalid</em> etc.</p>

<p>It turns out that as long as the blemish reaches only one portion of six — which is a sixth — and has not yet touched further, which is the aspect of having blemished one of the six aspects — for example, the aspect of <em>kosher</em> — and it has not yet touched further into the boundary of <em>invalid</em>: it turns out that <em>kosher</em> has not yet been mixed with <em>invalid</em>, only that it is close to there. And therefore it is returnable.</p>

<p>But as soon as the blemish reaches further — that is, <strong>more than a sixth</strong> — it turns out that <em>kosher</em> has been mixed with <em>invalid</em>. For as soon as it touches more than a sixth, it touches <em>invalid</em>, and it is the aspect of mixing <em>kosher</em> with <em>invalid</em>. And therefore the transaction is completely void <span class="bracket">[<span class="key-term">bitul mekach</span>]</span>, for the transaction was only for the purpose of refining <span class="bracket">[the sparks]</span>; and since, on the contrary, through that transaction they became even more mixed — therefore it is completely void.</p>

<p>But when it is <strong>less than a sixth</strong>, it is not considered at all. For less than one portion of six is not considered anything there, for there are only <span class="hebrew-letter">ו</span> <span class="bracket">[six]</span> aspects, as mentioned above.</p>

<hr class="divider">

<!-- TRANSLATOR'S SUMMARY -->

<div class="summary-box">
<h4>&#x1F4D6; Translator&rsquo;s Summary</h4>

<p><strong>Onaah 1</strong> gives the foundational framework for the laws of price-fraud, based on Likutay Moharan I:280. The core idea: commerce (<em>masa umattan</em>) exists to refine holy sparks (<em>nitzozos</em>) — to separate good from bad. This refinement is powered by <em>emunah</em> (faith), which draws its strength from the Torah. The Torah, represented by <span class="hebrew-letter">ו</span> (<em>vav</em> = 6), corresponds to the World of Formation (<em>Yetzirah</em>) and the angel <em>Matatron</em>, who embodies the six orders of the <em>Mishnah</em> — the system that clarifies: <em>kosher/invalid</em>, <em>pure/impure</em>, <em>permitted/forbidden</em>.</p>

<p>When fraud occurs, <em>emunah</em> is blemished and the sparks cannot be refined — good gets mixed with bad instead of being separated. Since the Torah-system has exactly six categories (three pairs), the measure of fraud is calibrated to a sixth:</p>

<p><strong>Exactly 1/6:</strong> The blemish has reached the boundary of one category (e.g. <em>kosher</em>) but hasn&rsquo;t crossed into its opposite (<em>invalid</em>) — so return/repair is still possible.</p>

<p><strong>More than 1/6:</strong> The blemish has crossed the boundary — <em>kosher</em> is now mixed with <em>invalid</em>. The transaction is void, because instead of refining, it made things worse.</p>

<p><strong>Less than 1/6:</strong> The blemish hasn&rsquo;t even reached one full portion of six — it doesn&rsquo;t register in the system at all.</p>
</div>

